{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"body":"Plan step 1 measurements, 2026-10-31, on a copy of the live bindings.json (23.5 MB, 5,664 bindings), origin/main d74d53a5ffd, each run in its own process, tracemalloc peak:\n- Top-level keys: bindings 22.16 MB serialized (94%), comment_ids 0.24 MB, reverse 0.20 MB (0.8%), version. Writer is binding_repository.py json.dump(indent=2, sort_keys=True) via mkstemp + os.replace.\n- Current binding_jira_key_map: 67.1 MB peak, 0.11 s (2 runs). Reproduces the observation (66.4 MB on the 22.1 MB file).\n- Prototype A, format-coupled seek (mmap, rfind the top-level indent-2 reverse key, raw_decode only that value): 1.3 MB peak, 0.017 s, identical map. Behaviour difference found: a file with a git conflict marker inserted inside the bindings section returns {} today and the full 5,664-entry map under A, so the documented corrupt-store -> {} contract does not hold.\n- Prototype B, full validating parse with an object_pairs_hook that discards every non-leaf object: 47.0 MB peak, 0.11 s, identical map, corrupt-store contract kept.\n- Option C, per-process cache keyed on (mtime_ns, size): first call per process is the current 67.1 MB; later calls in the same process skip the parse until the file changes. CLI searches are one call per process, so C changes nothing there; it helps the long-lived MCP server.\nPer-call loads: search_state calls binding_jira_key_map once per search; resolve_ticket_id loads the file once per Jira-key-shaped id (_JIRA_KEY_RE), including search's parent_resolver when the parent filter is a Jira key.\nThe remedy choice is paused pending an operator decision: A trades the corrupt-store contract for the largest cut, B keeps the contract for a smaller cut, C keeps the contract and only removes repeat loads."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793476691633832001,"uuid":"2a683a63-fd81-4150-afa5-7826b8bf86f6"}